WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.452 --> 00:00:03.594
- [Voiceover] This is
the Blanding's turtle.

00:00:03.594 --> 00:00:07.620
Look closely because they're
hard to spot in the wild.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.869
Blanding's turtles aren't very big,

00:00:09.869 --> 00:00:12.951
topping out at around
ten inches in length.

00:00:12.951 --> 00:00:15.525
What distinguishes them is
their bright yellow chin

00:00:15.525 --> 00:00:16.446
and throat.

00:00:16.446 --> 00:00:20.869
Like the stereotype, Blanding's
turtles take their time.

00:00:20.869 --> 00:00:23.338
However, once they've
reached full adulthood,

00:00:23.338 --> 00:00:25.297
there is no slowing down.

00:00:25.297 --> 00:00:28.157
A recent study found an 83 year old female

00:00:28.157 --> 00:00:30.884
that was full of eggs ready to lay.

00:00:30.884 --> 00:00:34.369
Their general habitat is
wetlands with shallow water.

00:00:34.369 --> 00:00:36.621
Their range is centered
around the Great Lakes,

00:00:36.621 --> 00:00:40.839
extending east to New York,
and up to southern Ontario.

00:00:40.839 --> 00:00:43.549
But the wetlands they
call home are shrinking

00:00:43.549 --> 00:00:46.351
and becoming fragmented
from human development,

00:00:46.351 --> 00:00:48.791
the biggest threat to the
survival of this gentle

00:00:48.791 --> 00:00:51.103
and timid creature.

00:00:51.103 --> 00:00:52.733
This is the Blanding's turtle.

00:00:52.733 --> 00:00:55.316
(chimes music)

